{% comment %}
  The two product cards that sit just under the hero.

  Styled after the packaging-card artwork: an outlined card in the brand
  terracotta, the product name across the top in the heading serif, the photo
  inset below it, and a solid call-to-action bar flush to the bottom edge.

  Two things worth knowing before editing:

  1. The image ratio is a real setting and defaults to 4:3, which is the chai
     photo's native ratio and only trims the sides of the coffee one. Leaving
     it on a shared ratio is what keeps both cards' bars on one baseline.
  2. The bar is a link by default, because the store has no products yet and a
     bar reading "add to cart" that adds nothing is a lie. Pick a product in
     the block and it becomes a real add-to-cart form instead - no other
     change needed.
{% endcomment %}

{%- comment -%}
  The spice field rides on the theme's own section-background element rather
  than on a pseudo-element of the content div: that element is already exactly
  viewport-wide and absolutely positioned, so the artwork gets its full bleed
  for free, where a 100vw pseudo-element adds 9px of horizontal overflow on
  mobile (100vw counts the scrollbar).

  The art is Santosh's "KCC All" drawing - one scattered composition, not a
  tile - so it is sized to cover rather than repeated. The URL has to be handed
  to the CSS from here: {% stylesheet %} is static and never sees Liquid, so
  asset_url cannot be written inside it.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  id="product-teaser-bg-{{ section.id }}"
  class="section-background color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}{% if section.settings.show_backdrop %} product-teaser__bg{% endif %}"
  {%- if section.settings.show_backdrop %}
    style="--pt-scatter: url('{{ 'ingredient-scatter.svg' | asset_url }}'); --pt-scatter-opacity: {{ section.settings.backdrop_opacity | divided_by: 100.0 }};"
    data-scatter-src="{{ 'ingredient-scatter.svg' | asset_url }}"
  {%- endif %}
></div>
<div
  class="section section--{{ section.settings.section_width }} color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} product-teaser spacing-style"
  style="{%- render 'spacing-style', settings: section.settings %}"
>
  {%- if section.settings.eyebrow != blank or section.settings.heading != blank -%}
    <div class="product-teaser__head">
      {%- if section.settings.eyebrow != blank -%}
        <p class="product-teaser__eyebrow">{{ section.settings.eyebrow }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- if section.settings.heading != blank -%}
        <h2 class="product-teaser__heading">{{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- liquid
    case section.settings.image_ratio
      when 'square'
        assign ratio = '1'
      when 'three-two'
        assign ratio = '3 / 2'
      when 'four-three'
        assign ratio = '4 / 3'
    endcase
  -%}

  <ul
    class="product-teaser__grid"
    role="list"
    style="--pt-max: {{ section.settings.max_width }}rem;"
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      Which side each card comes in from. The ones drawn on the left half of
      the row arrive from the left and the right half from the right, so a
      pair converges on its own layout instead of queueing in from one edge.
      The entrance itself is the theme's shared one (assets/kcc-reveal.js);
      this only names the direction.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign halfway = section.blocks.size | divided_by: 2.0 -%}
    {%- for block in section.blocks -%}
      {%- if block.type == 'product' -%}
        {%- if forloop.index0 < halfway -%}
          {%- assign side = 'left' -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- assign side = 'right' -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The slot and the card are two elements on purpose. The reveal's
          transition is set at a higher specificity than the card's own and
          stays applied afterwards, so with both on one element the 0.15s
          hover lift would inherit the entrance's 0.65s and crawl. The slot
          carries the entrance; the card keeps its own hover.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <li class="product-teaser__slot" data-kcc-rise="{{ side }}" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
         <div class="product-teaser__card">
          <div class="product-teaser__inner">
            {%- if block.settings.title != blank -%}
              <h3 class="product-teaser__title">{{ block.settings.title | escape }}</h3>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if block.settings.image != blank -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                `adapt` leaves --pt-ratio unset so the image keeps its own shape.
                Every other option pins both cards to the same ratio so their
                captions sit on a shared baseline.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <div
                class="product-teaser__media"
                {% if ratio %}style="--pt-ratio: {{ ratio }};"{% endif %}
              >
                <img
                  class="product-teaser__image"
                  src="{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 900 }}"
                  srcset="{{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 440 }} 440w, {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 660 }} 660w, {{ block.settings.image | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w"
                  sizes="(min-width: 750px) 26rem, 90vw"
                  alt="{{ block.settings.image.alt | escape }}"
                  width="{{ block.settings.image.width }}"
                  height="{{ block.settings.image.height }}"
                  loading="lazy"
                >
              </div>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if block.settings.body != blank -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                Escaped because these are plain-text settings, and the coffee
                copy contains a bare ampersand.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <p class="product-teaser__line">{{ block.settings.body | escape }}</p>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>

          {%- if block.settings.link_label != blank -%}
            {%- assign card_product = block.settings.product -%}
            {%- if card_product != blank and card_product.available -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                A plain POST to /cart/add, so the bar works without JavaScript.
                It lands on the cart page rather than opening the drawer.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <form method="post" action="{{ routes.cart_add_url }}" class="product-teaser__cta-form">
                <input type="hidden" name="form_type" value="product">
                <input type="hidden" name="utf8" value="✓">
                <input
                  type="hidden"
                  name="id"
                  value="{{ card_product.selected_or_first_available_variant.id }}"
                >
                <button
                  type="submit"
                  class="product-teaser__cta product-teaser__cta--{{ section.settings.cta_style }}"
                >
                  {{- block.settings.link_label -}}
                </button>
              </form>
            {%- else -%}
              <a
                class="product-teaser__cta product-teaser__cta--{{ section.settings.cta_style }}"
                href="{{ block.settings.link_url | default: '/collections/all' }}"
              >
                {{- block.settings.link_label -}}
              </a>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endif -%}
         </div>
        </li>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  </ul>
</div>

{%- if section.settings.show_backdrop and section.settings.backdrop_motion -%}
<script>
  (() => {
    /*
      The spice field, which moves on its own and parts around the pointer.

      Two systems share the artwork, on two layers, because they want very
      different things from the browser:

        .kcc-scatter__item    the pointer physics, written as a `transform`
                              attribute from a rAF loop that only runs while
                              something is still moving
        .kcc-scatter__wander  a long CSS wander, 7-15s
        .kcc-scatter__shiver  a short CSS shiver, 2.4-5s

      Nesting them is what lets the ambient motion keep running on the
      compositor while the physics sleeps, instead of the loop having to drive
      everything every frame forever. The field is therefore never still, and
      costs nothing per frame unless a pointer is actually in it.

      The physics is the engine from sections/ingredient-spotlight.liquid,
      which drives the same artwork from the same data attributes. The one
      substantive difference: there, the field is at rest between strokes and
      an icon's drawn centre is exactly where it is. Here the ambient motion
      has already carried it up to ~17 units away by the time the cursor
      arrives, and the physics still measures from the drawn centre. At a
      reach of 200 units that is an 8% error in which icons get pushed, which
      is not visible, and pricing it properly would mean reading 135 live
      matrices every frame.

      Progressive enhancement: the CSS background-image is what renders on
      load and is what stays if the fetch fails, if there is no JS, or if the
      visitor asks for reduced motion. The inlined SVG carries the same
      viewBox and preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice" as the CSS cover
      sizing, so it lands on the identical crop and the swap cannot be seen.

      Desktop only, deliberately. On a phone the field is tiled rather than
      covered (an inlined SVG cannot tile), the stacked cards leave almost
      none of it showing, there is no cursor to part it with, and a 566KB
      fetch is not worth that sliver.
    */
    const bg = document.getElementById('product-teaser-bg-{{ section.id }}');
    if (!bg || bg.dataset.kccReady) return;

    const src = bg.getAttribute('data-scatter-src');
    if (!src) return;

    const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
    const wide = window.matchMedia('(min-width: 750px)');
    const net = navigator.connection;
    if (net && (net.saveData || /(^|-)2g$/.test(net.effectiveType || ''))) return;

    const SVG_NS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';

    /*
      ---- the ambient half ----

      Two motions on two clocks: a long wander that carries an icon somewhere
      and back, and a short shiver on top of it. One alone is not enough - the
      wander by itself is too slow to read as movement at this strength, and
      the shiver by itself is a twitch in place.

      They go on separate nested groups so each can animate the `transform`
      shorthand. The obvious alternative is one element with two animations on
      the newer `translate` and `rotate` properties, which compose without any
      extra DOM - and it works, and it is four times slower. Measured on this
      page, 135 icons at 1440px wide: `transform` holds a 16.7ms median frame,
      the separate properties give 80ms. Chrome accelerates transform on an
      SVG group and does not accelerate those. Don't "simplify" this back.

      Distances are in the artwork's own user units - the viewBox is 2560x1440
      - so an icon travels the same distance relative to its neighbours at
      every width.
    */
    const AMP_MIN = 6;         // units
    const AMP_MAX = 17;
    const AMP_OF_INK = 0.075;  // share of an icon's own radius
    const WANDER_MIN = 7;      // seconds
    const WANDER_MAX = 15;
    const SPIN_MIN = 0.55;     // degrees, either way of rest
    const SPIN_MAX = 1.7;
    const SHIVER_MIN = 2.4;    // seconds
    const SHIVER_MAX = 5;

    /*
      ---- the pointer half ----

      All of this is in the artwork's own user units too, so the motion scales
      with the drawing instead of with the viewport.
    */
    const STEP = 1 / 60;
    /*
      Spring and damping make a lightly underdamped oscillator: a pushed icon
      swings out, comes back, overshoots a little and settles. SPRING sets how
      far a given push carries, DAMP how fast it dies out. Measured settle is
      about 2.5s, with one small overshoot.
    */
    const SPRING = 0.004;
    const DAMP = 0.943;
    const RESTITUTION = 0.92;
    const MAX_SPIN = 4.5;      // degrees
    const SPIN_SPRING = 0.012;
    const SPIN_DAMP = 0.95;
    const SLEEP_V = 0.004;
    const SLEEP_D = 0.3;
    // Leash, scaled to the icon's own size: a carom seed travelling as far as
    // a cinnamon stick would be moving further than its own width and read as
    // flying rather than nudged.
    const LEASH_MIN = 12;
    const LEASH_MAX = 34;
    /*
      REACH is how far from the cursor an icon has to be before it ignores it;
      200 units is about 110px across this band. The push is deliberately
      small: at full speed, directly under the cursor, a mid-sized icon swings
      about 20 units, which is ~11px on screen. Anything more and the backdrop
      stops being a backdrop.
    */
    const REACH = 200;
    const SPEED_REF = 60;      // units/frame that counts as a brisk flick
    const PUSH_MIN = 0.26;     // a slow drag still parts the field
    const PUSH_SPEED = 1;      // ...and a fast one parts it properly
    const SWEEP = 0.45;        // share of the push that follows the stroke
    const SWEEP_SPIN = 0.05;

    const between = (a, b) => a + Math.random() * (b - a);

    let items = [];
    let pairs = [];
    let raf = 0;
    let last = 0;
    let carry = 0;
    let loaded = false;
    let visible = false;
    let svgEl = null;

    /*
      Pointer position in the artwork's own coordinates. getScreenCTM is what
      does the mapping, because it already accounts for the slice crop and the
      page's scroll offset. It's cached and dropped on scroll/resize rather
      than read per event, since reading it forces layout.
    */
    const pointer = { x: 0, y: 0, prevX: 0, prevY: 0, fresh: false, seen: false };
    let ctm = null;
    const dropCTM = () => { ctm = null; };

    const toArt = (clientX, clientY) => {
      if (!svgEl) return null;
      if (!ctm) {
        const m = svgEl.getScreenCTM();
        if (!m) return null;
        ctm = m.inverse();
      }
      return new DOMPoint(clientX, clientY).matrixTransform(ctm);
    };

    /*
      Push everything near the segment the pointer covered since the last
      frame. Distance is to the segment, not to its end point: at 2000px/s the
      cursor can clear an icon's whole reach between two frames, and measuring
      against the end point alone would let it slip through untouched.
    */
    const sweep = () => {
      const ax = pointer.prevX;
      const ay = pointer.prevY;
      const dx = pointer.x - ax;
      const dy = pointer.y - ay;
      const seg = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
      // A jump this big is a scroll or a tab switch, not a stroke.
      if (seg > 700) return;
      const speed = Math.min(seg / SPEED_REF, 1);

      for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
        const it = items[i];
        const px = it.cx + it.ox;
        const py = it.cy + it.oy;

        let t = 0;
        if (seg > 1e-3) {
          t = ((px - ax) * dx + (py - ay) * dy) / (seg * seg);
          t = t < 0 ? 0 : t > 1 ? 1 : t;
        }
        let nx = px - (ax + dx * t);
        let ny = py - (ay + dy * t);
        let d = Math.hypot(nx, ny);
        if (d > REACH) continue;
        if (d < 1e-3) {
          // Dead centre: pick a direction rather than dividing by zero.
          const a = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
          nx = Math.cos(a);
          ny = Math.sin(a);
          d = 1;
        }

        const falloff = 1 - d / REACH;
        const f = falloff * falloff * (PUSH_MIN + PUSH_SPEED * speed) * (it.leash / 24);
        it.vx += (nx / d) * f;
        it.vy += (ny / d) * f;

        if (seg > 1e-3) {
          // A fraction of the push goes along the direction of travel rather
          // than straight away from the cursor, so the field parts and trails
          // behind the stroke instead of bulging out of a point.
          it.vx += (dx / seg) * f * SWEEP;
          it.vy += (dy / seg) * f * SWEEP;
          // Which side of the stroke it sits on decides which way it turns.
          it.va += ((nx * dy - ny * dx) / (d * seg)) * f * SWEEP_SPIN;
        }
        it.asleep = false;
      }
    };

    /*
      Give every icon its two ambient clocks and its two nested groups, and
      read out what the physics needs. One pass, once, at inline time.
    */
    const build = (svg) => {
      svgEl = svg;
      const nodes = svg.querySelectorAll('.kcc-scatter__item');
      if (!nodes.length) return false;

      items = Array.from(nodes).map((el) => {
        const r = parseFloat(el.getAttribute('data-r')) || 40;

        const wanderG = document.createElementNS(SVG_NS, 'g');
        wanderG.setAttribute('class', 'kcc-scatter__wander');
        const shiverG = document.createElementNS(SVG_NS, 'g');
        shiverG.setAttribute('class', 'kcc-scatter__shiver');
        while (el.firstChild) shiverG.appendChild(el.firstChild);
        wanderG.appendChild(shiverG);
        el.appendChild(wanderG);

        // Amplitude scaled to the icon's own ink: a carom seed travels a
        // seed's worth and a cinnamon stick a stick's worth. A flat amplitude
        // makes small ink look like it is flying and big ink look stuck.
        const amp = Math.min(AMP_MAX, Math.max(AMP_MIN, r * AMP_OF_INK));
        const angle = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
        const wander = between(WANDER_MIN, WANDER_MAX);
        const shiver = between(SHIVER_MIN, SHIVER_MAX);
        const spin = between(SPIN_MIN, SPIN_MAX) * (Math.random() < 0.5 ? -1 : 1);

        wanderG.style.setProperty('--jx', (Math.cos(angle) * amp).toFixed(2) + 'px');
        wanderG.style.setProperty('--jy', (Math.sin(angle) * amp).toFixed(2) + 'px');
        wanderG.style.animationDuration = wander.toFixed(2) + 's';
        // Negative, so every icon starts mid-cycle. Without it the whole
        // field sets off from rest together on the first frame.
        wanderG.style.animationDelay = (-Math.random() * wander).toFixed(2) + 's';

        shiverG.style.setProperty('--jr', spin.toFixed(2) + 'deg');
        shiverG.style.animationDuration = shiver.toFixed(2) + 's';
        shiverG.style.animationDelay = (-Math.random() * shiver).toFixed(2) + 's';

        /*
          Mass from the ink's footprint, so a cinnamon stick shrugs off a
          carom seed instead of the two trading equal momentum. Clamped at
          both ends: raw r spans 17 to 554 here (the big ginger root's bbox
          swallows half a quadrant), and a literal r^2 would make that root
          immovable, which reads as a bug rather than as weight.
        */
        const m = Math.pow(Math.min(Math.max(r, 24), 200), 1.5);
        return {
          el,
          cx: parseFloat(el.getAttribute('data-cx')) || 0,
          cy: parseFloat(el.getAttribute('data-cy')) || 0,
          r,
          inv: 1 / m,
          leash: Math.min(Math.max(r * 0.5, LEASH_MIN), LEASH_MAX),
          ox: 0, oy: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0,
          ang: 0, va: 0,
          asleep: true,
          dirty: false,
        };
      });

      pairs = [];
      for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
        for (let j = i + 1; j < items.length; j++) {
          const a = items[i];
          const b = items[j];
          const d0 = Math.hypot(a.cx - b.cx, a.cy - b.cy);
          const contact = a.r + b.r;
          /*
            Clusters in the drawing deliberately overlap - the pile of coffee
            beans, the ginger root sitting over its neighbours. If the resting
            distance were just a.r + b.r those pairs would start inside each
            other and the solver would blow Santosh's composition apart on the
            first frame. So a pair's rest distance is capped at how far apart
            it was actually drawn: it only pushes back once the two are closer
            than the artist put them.
          */
          const rest = Math.min(contact, d0 * 0.9);
          // Only pairs that could ever touch, given how far each can drift.
          if (d0 < contact + a.leash + b.leash + 4) pairs.push({ a, b, rest });
        }
      }

      // Starts at rest. Nothing is simulated until a pointer crosses the
      // field; the ambient motion is the CSS underneath and needs no loop.
      return true;
    };

    const collide = () => {
      for (let k = 0; k < pairs.length; k++) {
        const { a, b, rest } = pairs[k];
        if (a.asleep && b.asleep) continue;
        const dx = (b.cx + b.ox) - (a.cx + a.ox);
        const dy = (b.cy + b.oy) - (a.cy + a.oy);
        const d = Math.hypot(dx, dy);
        if (d >= rest || d < 1e-4) continue;

        const nx = dx / d;
        const ny = dy / d;
        const invSum = a.inv + b.inv;

        // Ease them apart instead of snapping - a hard positional correction
        // on overlapping art pops visibly at this scale.
        const push = (rest - d) * 0.12;
        a.ox -= nx * push * (a.inv / invSum);
        a.oy -= ny * push * (a.inv / invSum);
        b.ox += nx * push * (b.inv / invSum);
        b.oy += ny * push * (b.inv / invSum);

        const rvx = b.vx - a.vx;
        const rvy = b.vy - a.vy;
        const vn = rvx * nx + rvy * ny;
        if (vn < 0) {
          const imp = (-(1 + RESTITUTION) * vn) / invSum;
          a.vx -= nx * imp * a.inv;
          a.vy -= ny * imp * a.inv;
          b.vx += nx * imp * b.inv;
          b.vy += ny * imp * b.inv;
          // Glancing hits spin things; head-on ones don't.
          const vt = rvx * -ny + rvy * nx;
          a.va -= vt * 0.5 * a.inv * 220;
          b.va += vt * 0.5 * b.inv * 220;
        }
        a.asleep = false;
        b.asleep = false;
      }
    };

    const integrate = () => {
      for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
        const it = items[i];
        if (it.asleep) continue;

        it.vx = (it.vx - it.ox * SPRING) * DAMP;
        it.vy = (it.vy - it.oy * SPRING) * DAMP;
        it.ox += it.vx;
        it.oy += it.vy;

        const d = Math.hypot(it.ox, it.oy);
        if (d > it.leash) {
          // Bounce off its own leash, so a run of shoves in one direction
          // can't walk an icon out of the composition.
          const s = it.leash / d;
          it.ox *= s;
          it.oy *= s;
          const nx = it.ox / it.leash;
          const ny = it.oy / it.leash;
          const vn = it.vx * nx + it.vy * ny;
          if (vn > 0) {
            it.vx -= 1.6 * vn * nx;
            it.vy -= 1.6 * vn * ny;
          }
        }

        it.va = (it.va - it.ang * SPIN_SPRING) * SPIN_DAMP;
        it.ang += it.va;
        if (it.ang > MAX_SPIN) { it.ang = MAX_SPIN; it.va = 0; }
        if (it.ang < -MAX_SPIN) { it.ang = -MAX_SPIN; it.va = 0; }

        it.dirty = true;
        if (
          Math.abs(it.vx) < SLEEP_V && Math.abs(it.vy) < SLEEP_V &&
          Math.abs(it.va) < SLEEP_V && d < SLEEP_D && Math.abs(it.ang) < 0.08
        ) {
          // Settle exactly back onto the drawn position. The ambient layers
          // underneath carry on from wherever their own clocks are, so the
          // hand-off is invisible.
          it.ox = it.oy = it.vx = it.vy = it.ang = it.va = 0;
          it.asleep = true;
        }
      }
    };

    const draw = () => {
      for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
        const it = items[i];
        if (!it.dirty) continue;
        it.dirty = false;
        if (it.asleep) {
          it.el.removeAttribute('transform');
        } else {
          it.el.setAttribute(
            'transform',
            'translate(' + it.ox.toFixed(2) + ',' + it.oy.toFixed(2) + ') rotate(' +
              it.ang.toFixed(2) + ',' + it.cx + ',' + it.cy + ')'
          );
        }
      }
    };

    const tick = (now) => {
      // A tab that was backgrounded comes back with a huge gap; don't
      // simulate it, just carry on from here.
      const dt = last ? Math.min((now - last) / 1000, 0.1) : STEP;
      last = now;

      if (pointer.fresh) {
        sweep();
        pointer.fresh = false;
        pointer.prevX = pointer.x;
        pointer.prevY = pointer.y;
      }

      carry = Math.min(carry + dt, 0.25);
      while (carry >= STEP) {
        collide();
        integrate();
        carry -= STEP;
      }
      draw();

      // Stop dead once the field has settled. The next pointer move starts it
      // again; until then the pointer half costs nothing per frame.
      let awake = 0;
      for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
        if (!items[i].asleep) awake++;
      }
      if (awake) {
        raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      } else {
        raf = 0;
        last = 0;
        carry = 0;
      }
    };

    const wake = () => {
      if (raf || !visible || !items.length) return;
      last = 0;
      carry = 0;
      raf = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
    };

    const onPointerMove = (e) => {
      if (!visible || !items.length) return;
      const p = toArt(e.clientX, e.clientY);
      if (!p) return;
      pointer.x = p.x;
      pointer.y = p.y;
      // The first sample after arriving has no stroke behind it, so treat it
      // as a standing start rather than a sweep from wherever it was last.
      if (!pointer.seen) {
        pointer.prevX = p.x;
        pointer.prevY = p.y;
        pointer.seen = true;
      }
      pointer.fresh = true;
      wake();
    };

    const play = () => {
      visible = true;
      dropCTM();
      bg.classList.add('is-live');
      wake();
    };

    const pause = () => {
      visible = false;
      pointer.seen = false;
      // Parks the CSS animations too (animation-play-state), so a visitor
      // three sections further down is not paying for any of this.
      bg.classList.remove('is-live');
      if (!raf) return;
      cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
      raf = 0;
    };

    const load = async () => {
      if (loaded) return;
      loaded = true;
      let markup;
      try {
        const res = await fetch(src);
        if (!res.ok) return;
        markup = await res.text();
      } catch {
        return;
      }
      const holder = document.createElement('div');
      holder.innerHTML = markup;
      const svg = holder.querySelector('svg');
      if (!svg || !build(svg)) return;
      svg.setAttribute('class', 'product-teaser__scatter');
      svg.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      svg.setAttribute('focusable', 'false');
      bg.appendChild(svg);
      bg.classList.add('is-scattered');
    };

    // Nothing is fetched until the band is near the viewport.
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        const onScreen = entries.some((e) => e.isIntersecting);
        if (onScreen && !reduceMotion.matches && wide.matches) {
          load().then(() => { if (items.length) play(); });
        } else {
          pause();
        }
      },
      { rootMargin: '200px 0px' }
    );
    io.observe(bg);

    window.addEventListener('pointermove', onPointerMove, { passive: true });
    // Both move the artwork under the cursor without a pointer event firing,
    // which would leave the cached mapping pointing at the wrong place.
    window.addEventListener('scroll', dropCTM, { passive: true });
    window.addEventListener('resize', dropCTM, { passive: true });

    // A window dragged narrow, or a visitor who turns motion off mid-visit,
    // is a new answer to the same question.
    const recheck = () => {
      if (!reduceMotion.matches && wide.matches) return;
      pause();
      items.forEach((it) => {
        it.ox = it.oy = it.vx = it.vy = it.ang = it.va = 0;
        it.asleep = true;
        it.el.removeAttribute('transform');
      });
    };
    reduceMotion.addEventListener?.('change', recheck);
    wide.addEventListener?.('change', recheck);

    bg.dataset.kccReady = 'true';
  })();
</script>
{%- endif -%}
{% stylesheet %}
  .product-teaser {
    /* The card frame, the title and the bar are all one terracotta. Pulled
       from the scheme so a scheme swap carries the whole card with it. */
    --pt-accent: var(--color-primary, #cd643f);
    --pt-accent-deep: var(--color-primary-hover, #a45032);
    --pt-ink: #754B2A;
    --pt-radius: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* The spice field behind the cards.

     The section is a cream band between two white ones, so the field's top and
     bottom edges land on colour edges that are already there - no fade and no
     bleed needed. (The ingredient section below learned the other way round:
     fading an edge out hollowed the section, so it bleeds past instead.)

     Kept to a watermark. The cards are solid white and punch straight through
     it; what the field is for is the empty ground either side of them, which
     at this width is more than half the band. */
  .product-teaser__bg {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .product-teaser__bg::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background-image: var(--pt-scatter);
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    background-position: center;
    background-size: cover;
    opacity: var(--pt-scatter-opacity, 0.1);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* The inlined copy, once the script has it. Same viewBox and the same
     xMidYMid slice as the CSS `cover` above, so it lands on the identical
     crop and the swap is invisible. */
  .product-teaser__scatter {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    opacity: var(--pt-scatter-opacity, 0.1);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Two layers of the same ink at 13% would read as 24%. */
  .product-teaser__bg.is-scattered::after {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
     .kcc-scatter__item is left alone on purpose. The pointer engine writes a
     `transform` attribute on it, with its own rotation centre baked into the
     rotate(), and setting transform-box/transform-origin here would apply a
     second origin shift on top of that and throw every push sideways. The
     ambient motion therefore lives on the two groups nested inside it.

     fill-box on those, so each icon turns about its own centre rather than
     about the corner of a 2560x1440 viewBox, which would swing it clean
     across the band.
  */
  .product-teaser__scatter .kcc-scatter__wander,
  .product-teaser__scatter .kcc-scatter__shiver {
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: 50% 50%;
    animation-timing-function: ease-in-out;
    animation-iteration-count: infinite;
    animation-play-state: paused;
  }

  .product-teaser__scatter .kcc-scatter__wander {
    animation-name: kcc-pt-wander;
  }

  .product-teaser__scatter .kcc-scatter__shiver {
    animation-name: kcc-pt-shiver;
  }

  .product-teaser__bg.is-live .kcc-scatter__wander,
  .product-teaser__bg.is-live .kcc-scatter__shiver {
    animation-play-state: running;
  }

  /* Four stops rather than two: a there-and-back is a pendulum, and a field
     of pendulums reads as a mechanism. The uneven offsets and the asymmetric
     scalings give each icon a path that doesn't retrace itself. */
  @keyframes kcc-pt-wander {
    0%,
    100% {
      transform: translate(0, 0);
    }

    22% {
      transform: translate(var(--jx, 0px), var(--jy, 0px));
    }

    48% {
      transform: translate(calc(var(--jx, 0px) * -0.72), calc(var(--jy, 0px) * 0.48));
    }

    73% {
      transform: translate(calc(var(--jx, 0px) * 0.42), calc(var(--jy, 0px) * -0.8));
    }
  }

  /* The fast one is allowed to be a pendulum: a shiver is oscillation, and
     with 135 different periods and phases over it nothing reads as in step. */
  @keyframes kcc-pt-shiver {
    0%,
    100% {
      transform: rotate(calc(var(--jr, 0deg) * -1));
    }

    50% {
      transform: rotate(var(--jr, 0deg));
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .product-teaser__scatter .kcc-scatter__wander,
    .product-teaser__scatter .kcc-scatter__shiver {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  /* On a phone the cards stack, so the band is tall and narrow and `cover`
     scales the drawing by its height - one ginger root ends up filling the
     strip beside a card. Sized and tiled instead, so the ink stays about the
     size it is on a laptop and the narrow gaps read as a field rather than as
     a fragment. The drawing isn't a seamless tile, but at this strength, in
     strips this narrow, the joins don't show - checked on the page. */
  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .product-teaser__bg::after {
      background-size: 760px auto;
      background-repeat: repeat;
    }
  }

  .product-teaser__head {
    text-align: center;
    margin-block-end: 2.5rem;
  }

  .product-teaser__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--pt-accent);
  }

  .product-teaser__heading {
    font-family: var(--font-heading--family);
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 3vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* Capped and centred rather than filling the page. At full width each card
     was over 40% of a laptop viewport, which read as two billboards instead of
     a pair of products. */
  .product-teaser__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 2rem);
    /* Explicit, because `.section` sizes its children by content and the
       cards are inline-size containers - their max-content contribution is
       zero, so the row would shrink to the bare 16rem track minimums. */
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--pt-max, 54rem);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .product-teaser__grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    }
  }

  /* The grid cell. Grid rather than block so the card stretches to the row's
     height without being told to. */
  .product-teaser__slot {
    display: grid;
  }

  /* Outlined rather than shadowed at rest: the frame is the look. `overflow`
     is what crops the bar into the two bottom corners. */
  .product-teaser__card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    border: 2px solid var(--pt-accent);
    border-radius: var(--pt-radius);
    overflow: hidden;
    /* White rather than var(--color-background): the section sits on the cream
       scheme, so the variable would resolve to the same cream and the card
       would disappear against it. */
    background: #fff;
    /* So the title can be sized off the card rather than the viewport. */
    container-type: inline-size;
    transition: transform 0.15s ease, box-shadow 0.15s ease;
  }

  .product-teaser__card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    box-shadow: 0 0.75rem 1.75rem rgb(43 22 8 / 0.12);
  }

  /* `flex: 1` so a one-line description doesn't leave that card's bar riding
     higher than its neighbour's. */
  .product-teaser__inner {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex: 1;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    padding: clamp(0.875rem, 5cqi, 1.5rem);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .product-teaser__title {
    font-family: var(--font-heading--family);
    /* Measured off the artwork: the name is 11.7% of the card's width there,
       which is why it reads as a nameplate rather than a caption. Sized in
       cqi so it stays that proportion on a phone as well as a laptop. */
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 11.5cqi, 3rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--pt-accent);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* Hollow, like the packaging artwork. Same font - Noto Serif Display Extra
     Condensed, already loaded in layout/theme.liquid - with the fill dropped
     and the contour stroked instead, the way the nav thickens on hover.

     The stroke is centred on the outline, so it eats into the stem rather
     than growing the word: the metrics stay the filled font's and nothing
     reflows. 0.038em is the artwork's ratio, and em keeps it true at every
     size. Guarded, because without text-stroke the transparent fill would
     leave the title invisible rather than merely solid. */
  @supports (-webkit-text-stroke: 1px currentColor) {
    .product-teaser__title {
      color: transparent;
      -webkit-text-stroke: 0.038em var(--pt-accent);
    }
  }

  /* Windows high-contrast throws the stroke away but keeps the fill, so the
     fill has to come back. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .product-teaser__title {
      color: CanvasText;
      -webkit-text-stroke-width: 0;
    }
  }

  .product-teaser__media {
    aspect-ratio: var(--pt-ratio, auto);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .product-teaser__image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .product-teaser__line {
    margin: 0;
    /* Keeps the description hugging the photo while the gap it would have
       taken goes under it, above the bar. */
    margin-block-end: auto;
    /* 15px on a phone is where this description was, and it is the only
       sentence that says what is in the jar. */
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.2vw, 1.0625rem);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--pt-ink);
  }

  .product-teaser__cta-form {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
  }

  /* The solid bar, flush to the card's three lower edges. */
  .product-teaser__cta {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0.875rem 1rem;
    background: var(--pt-accent);
    color: #fff;
    font-family: var(--font-body--family);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.6vw, 1.1875rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.2;
    text-align: center;
    text-decoration: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: background-color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .product-teaser__cta:hover,
  .product-teaser__cta:focus-visible {
    background: var(--pt-accent-deep);
    color: #fff;
  }

  /* The quieter form, for a card that shouldn't shout. */
  .product-teaser__cta--link {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--pt-accent);
    padding-block: 0 clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.75rem);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-underline-offset: 0.3em;
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .product-teaser__cta--link:hover,
  .product-teaser__cta--link:focus-visible {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--pt-accent-deep);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .product-teaser__card {
      transition: none;
    }

    .product-teaser__card:hover {
      transform: none;
    }
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

